Q: Specifying signal delays in SystemC as clause AFTER in VHDL A: <p>If you are inside an SC_METHOD or if you don't want to stop (in time) an SC_THREAD, then the following will work:</p>
<pre><code>#define SC_INCLUDE_DYNAMIC_PROCESSES
#include &lt;systemc&gt;

  //...
  sc_signal&lt;int, SC_MANY_WRITERS&gt; sig;
  //...
  sc_spawn( [&amp;](){ wait(DELAY); sig.write(VALUE); } );
</code></pre>
<p>This spawns a new temporary SC_THREAD that does nothing but wait for the DELAY and then write the signal. SC_MANY_WRITERS is necessary here, because the signal is written by multiple SystemC processes (i.e. SC_THREADs and SC_METHODs), which would lead to an error message without this flag (only a single SystemC process would be allowed to write in that case).</p>
<p>The solution above was taken from here: <a href="https://forums.accellera.org/topic/7010-how-to-model-output-delay-in-systemc-like-the-verilog-non-blocking-intra-assignment-delay/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://forums.accellera.org/topic/7010-how-to-model-output-delay-in-systemc-like-the-verilog-non-blocking-intra-assignment-delay/</a></p>
